Sure, I'm glad the code is working well for you! In Java, the `Map` interface represents an object that maps keys to values. It provides methods to add, remove, and retrieve values by their keys. There are various types of `Map` implementations available in Java, such as `HashMap`, `TreeMap`, `LinkedHashMap`, and others, each with its own advantages and disadvantages. In this program, we are using a `TreeMap`. The main advantage of a `TreeMap` is that it maintains the key-value pairs in sorted order based on the natural ordering of the keys (or an explicit `Comparator`). This makes it easy to list the words in alphabetical order, as we did in the program's output. Regarding performance, one possible improvement is to use a `HashSet` instead of a `TreeMap` to store the word counts. A `HashSet` is a collection that does not allow duplicate elements, and it provides `O(1)` time complexity for add, remove, and contains operations. In this program, we could first add all the words starting with the given letter to a `HashSet`, then count the occurrences of each word in the original list using a `HashMap`, and finally iterate over the `HashSet` to display the words in alphabetical order. Here's an example implementation of this approach:

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util. *;

public class WordCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        String letter = "";
        while (!letter.equalsIgnoreCase("quit")) {
            System.out.print("Enter a letter to search for or 'quit' to exit: ");
            letter = scanner.nextLine();
            if (letter.equalsIgnoreCase("quit")) {
                break;
            }
            String fileName = "input.txt"; // the name of the text file to read
            Set<String> wordSet = new HashSet<>(); // a set to store unique words
            Map<String, Integer> wordCounts = new HashMap<>(); // a map to store word counts

            try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName))) {
                String line;
                while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                    line = line.replaceAll("\\p{Punct}", ""); // remove all punctuation marks
                    String[] words = line.split("\\s+"); // split the line into words
                    for (String word : words) {
                        if (word.toLowerCase().startsWith(letter.toLowerCase())) { // check if the word starts with the given letter
                            wordSet.add(word);
                        }
                        wordCounts.put(word.toLowerCase(), wordCounts.getOrDefault(word.toLowerCase(), 0) + 1); // update word counts
                    }
                }
            } catch (IOException e) {
                System.err.println("Error reading file: " + e.getMessage());
                System.exit(1);
            }

            List<String> sortedWords = new ArrayList<>(wordSet);
            Collections.sort(sortedWords); // sort the words in alphabetical order
            int totalCount = 0;
            System.out.println("Words starting with " + letter.toUpperCase() + ":");
            for (String word : sortedWords) {
                int count = wordCounts.get(word.toLowerCase());
                System.out.println(word + " - " + count);
                totalCount += count;
            }
            System.out.println("Total count: " + totalCount);
        }
    }
}
```

